From 285465b70369aecacedb49c972151529191ed043 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 05:29:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(kv-offload): name the block in each disk slot header

Upstream base: the patch applies to local-inference-lab/vllm at
19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354 after the 702 patches of
spark-ds41f-1120 (candidate-62, 0001 to 0829) and patches 9030 to 9038
of this series.

Rationale: a later read of the code showed that the expected generation
of a slot is the latest store of the rank to that slot, so a slot that
was rewritten for another block between the lookup and the load still
passed the header check. With this patch, every store and every load of
the disk tier carries a block key for each slot, which is an 8-byte
BLAKE2b digest of the block's hash together with the block's group id
(disk_backend.slot_key). For a store the scheduler takes the keys from
the hashes of the stored GPU blocks, and for a load it takes them from
the CPU blocks that the lookup found, at the time of the lookup. The
slot header (version 2, 40 bytes in the slot padding) stores the key,
and the load compares it after the slot id and the generation and before
any copy to the GPU. A mismatch is an EBADMSG fault, so the load fails
on that rank, the request is a miss on all ranks, and the tier degrades
as it does for any other disk fault. CPU mode sends no keys. The key is
a digest and not a plain 8-byte prefix of the hash, because the hash of
a block that spans two hash blocks starts with the hash of the smaller
group's block at the same position, and the group id sits in the last 4
bytes. The key comes from the hash and not from a per-slot store count
in the scheduler, because the hash survives an engine restart, so a
persisted index could check a header without keeping a count; that index
is a design for a later change and is not part of this series.

Quality implications: a correct load gives the same output as before,
and a slot that holds another block now fails as a cache miss instead of
serving the wrong block.

Tests: on the CPU path, with VLLM_TARGET_DEVICE=cpu, the offload suite
passed 177 tests, with 2 CUDA-only modules skipped. The new file
test_ds41_tier_keys.py holds 5 tests. They check that keys separate the
groups, that a slot rewritten for another key fails a load that expects
the first key even when the generation matches, and that a matching key
loads. One test runs a disk-mode scheduler with a real worker as rank 1
of three: the second slot is rewritten between the lookup and the load
and fails, the first slot loads, the aggregator releases the request as
a miss, and the tier degrades. The last test checks that CPU mode sends
no keys. On DGX Spark hardware the patch ran only as part of the whole
series.

Upstream status: the patch is proposed to spark-ds41f in
experiments/2026-11-22-kv-disk-tier and has not been submitted to
local-inference-lab/vllm.
